#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221114 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d2b186-64d2-5ad8-b168-6c7bea413df3" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using Euler's Method for Second-Order ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "81a8347f-7852-5413-8d96-946ec658929e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21664" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + 4xy' - 3y = 2x, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad y'(0) = -2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z = y'$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = 1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nUse the information above to approximate the value of $y(1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-3y & =2x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-4x{y}^{′}+3y+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y', z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = -4xz + 3y + 2x.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(0) = y'(0) = -2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(0)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=-4xz+3y+2x,\\, & z(0)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=-2,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=-2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-4xz+3y+2x \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0+3+0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $3$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-2)=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+3=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx -1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221117 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd3b7856-4ac5-5473-a585-949dd06100e8" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/id #uuid "62004d73-b9ca-5133-b016-e2694a55211c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21668" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx^2y'' + 2y' = 4x^2, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\qquad y'(1) = -1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$ and $y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′} & =4{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y', z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{4x^2 - 2z}{x^2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1) = y'(1) = -1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and $y'=z$ at $x=\\dfrac32,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac32 - 1 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac32$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx -\\dfrac32$\nand\n$y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221118 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/id #uuid "25d6bf3b-282e-5c8b-b665-923dd602849d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21669" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n2x y'' - (y')^2 + 4y = 0, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\quad y'(1) = 2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and $y'(3).$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{″}-({y}^{′})^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ 2x{y}^{″} & =({y}^{′})^{2}-4y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{({y}^{′})^{2}-4y}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y',$ $z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{z^2 - 4y}{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1) = y'(1) = 2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{{z}^{2}-4y}{2x},\\, & z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z=y'$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3 - 1}2 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{{z}^{2}-4y}{2x}=\\frac{{2}^{2}-4(-1)}{2\\cdot 1}=4 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+2=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+4=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=6 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{{z}^{2}-4y}{2x}=\\frac{{6}^{2}-4\\cdot 1}{2\\cdot 2}=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot 1=6 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+6=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=6+8=14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ | $6$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $14$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3) \\approx 7$\nand\n$y'(3) = z(3) \\approx 14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd3b7856-4ac5-5473-a585-949dd06100e8" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f87f4c-67e4-584a-8b28-659e0826615f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86f87f4c-67e4-584a-8b28-659e0826615f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369378} :answer-field/id #uuid "09a8cd04-2f26-5e5d-a576-d02938b0d056" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220310} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53df8caa-11c0-5ed7-b820-3fe4e58e929d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53df8caa-11c0-5ed7-b820-3fe4e58e929d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369379} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a58a557-6e3d-5674-ac1a-19bb8ed43152" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "585a6b31-349e-5195-b6c5-30ce9e2bf96b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332989" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′}=2x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{5}{4})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\n$y(\\frac{5}{4})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4})≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′} & =2x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2x-3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2x-3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=0, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{4}-1=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2x-3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{5}{4})≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4})=z(\\frac{5}{4})≈-\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8618804a-4b7c-5789-b925-56ea9f7e57a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220329} {:answer/id #uuid "d97e13c4-07dd-5049-aebb-63791f485843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220330} {:answer/id #uuid "3971dca9-8c74-5120-a2a9-36a4fa0b8043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d97e13c4-07dd-5049-aebb-63791f485843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220330} :answer-field/id #uuid "f464a18e-9605-5490-9e7d-f8558d0ac485" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb3c8b5f-6f96-5815-95b9-f941928bbdd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220333} {:answer/id #uuid "271b9473-2e1e-5926-9899-aeb75a741a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220334} {:answer/id #uuid "42479b77-6dfe-5f3b-ad4c-a613c849ab28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb3c8b5f-6f96-5815-95b9-f941928bbdd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220333} :answer-field/id #uuid "cde5ad59-ed92-59e2-b6b7-ad6b896c6dfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f387b360-2a40-587c-949a-4f9384d87f91" :question/math-academy-id "q-333374" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =x{y}^{′}-y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{x{y}^{′}-y}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-2$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1-(-2)}{{1}^{2}}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{(3/2)\\cdot (5/2)-(-3/2)}{(-3/2)^{2}}=\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{7}{6}=\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $\\frac{11}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈-\\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{11}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d2b186-64d2-5ad8-b168-6c7bea413df3" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using Euler's Method for Second-Order ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f622f76e-3960-5305-8397-149876a7a9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f622f76e-3960-5305-8397-149876a7a9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369382} :answer-field/id #uuid "203922dd-032b-5316-8483-ec8df98bdae1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369381} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bafd6110-7366-5164-aeb4-c76c9dd99b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bafd6110-7366-5164-aeb4-c76c9dd99b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369384} :answer-field/id #uuid "509c8c3c-2c36-5956-b280-4ba0147c5128" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369383} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b1d355a-5e44-5687-8d93-a196c43d3a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b1d355a-5e44-5687-8d93-a196c43d3a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369386} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ef61da5-d7d0-5715-b27e-360951fa3f1b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99712f98-d725-55a8-9b6c-fc6d9fa02bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333506" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′}=4x,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′} & =4x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =4x-3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4x-3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{4x-3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4x-3yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=1,$ we place $y=2$ and $z=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4x-3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-6}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+1=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-2)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d2b186-64d2-5ad8-b168-6c7bea413df3" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using Euler's Method for Second-Order ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6b9049f-5dae-54b6-8c12-891a62407c51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369389} {:answer/id #uuid "5da2e073-d67a-531b-8a4b-1e869016cddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5da2e073-d67a-531b-8a4b-1e869016cddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369390} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b0d1d6-3051-52c4-9c93-c87d82c5523e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369388} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f563b83a-696d-56de-a045-076f2fc6dff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f563b83a-696d-56de-a045-076f2fc6dff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369392} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b0ca8b-b680-520e-8949-8f713c19df03" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c16c8198-0410-528d-90a5-c44986df246a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c16c8198-0410-528d-90a5-c44986df246a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369394} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bccbed6-4ad6-5baf-ac51-fd3fd9769fbe" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91de398b-f696-5215-92b6-62474e787634" :question/math-academy-id "q-333548" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+6y={x}^{2},\\,y(2)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(3)≈$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+6y & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =x{y}^{′}-6y+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=xz-6y+{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(2)={y}^{′}(2)=2.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(2)=-3, \\\\ {z}^{′}=xz-6y+{x}^{2},\\, & z(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(2)=-3$ and $z(2)=2,$ we place $y=-3$ and $z=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =xz-6y+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-6\\cdot (-3)+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4+18+4 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ |  | $2$ | $26$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =26\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $26$ | $26$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+2=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+26=28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $26$ | $26$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ |  |  | $28$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d2b186-64d2-5ad8-b168-6c7bea413df3" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using Euler's Method for Second-Order ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc68de2c-ca0c-5797-89b8-0a5005a97822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc68de2c-ca0c-5797-89b8-0a5005a97822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369397} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc744d93-61b7-5091-88d7-89820a64666c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397fdfad-7f6c-5ea8-af54-329b8f2728c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "397fdfad-7f6c-5ea8-af54-329b8f2728c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369399} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc91ade2-8564-5869-8c9e-9fb16a044c0b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369398} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e74e41-8499-580a-9086-d97c3e980b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e74e41-8499-580a-9086-d97c3e980b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369401} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3777f98-d74c-56c1-8519-0050cce63224" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63c79043-8111-562f-8014-2baa6cb9e25a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333622" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+4y{y}^{′}-3y=3{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+4y{y}^{′}-3y & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =3{x}^{2}-4y{y}^{′}+3y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4y{y}^{′}+3y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x},\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=0,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {1}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0+3}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+3=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d2b186-64d2-5ad8-b168-6c7bea413df3" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using Euler's Method for Second-Order ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7686e4a7-b839-5e99-b0fe-be0a5a107c0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369404} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6482dd-25df-59ea-8eda-4f123e63e582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc6482dd-25df-59ea-8eda-4f123e63e582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369405} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f5a5feb-0232-59fb-b3aa-e9e266b31347" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369403} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b06857d-c3bf-5e5d-a15b-799ddc1b2d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b06857d-c3bf-5e5d-a15b-799ddc1b2d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369407} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5893376-9b78-5bb9-a01d-c131da529e28" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369406} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9421076f-4fcb-5b1f-91ea-c1664d3427b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9421076f-4fcb-5b1f-91ea-c1664d3427b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369409} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d2c7e8-b9f3-5e19-9b5d-6acc20f0a35a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "956bac96-0834-53c7-a6be-443997b84a25" :question/math-academy-id "q-333653" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+3{y}^{2}={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+3{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}-2x{y}^{′}-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2},\\, & z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=2,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =1-4-3 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  | $2$ | $-6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+(-3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d2b186-64d2-5ad8-b168-6c7bea413df3" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using Euler's Method for Second-Order ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6e162f1-8c3c-5186-bb76-1095928e9850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6e162f1-8c3c-5186-bb76-1095928e9850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369412} :answer-field/id #uuid "33734d69-9d49-51f2-bd49-358a1a26082f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369411} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45683951-479a-531c-8259-8d97f027f1d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45683951-479a-531c-8259-8d97f027f1d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369414} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c5be30-7c01-5671-8baa-c3bbdfb324ea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e659d8d0-8e45-530b-8b4a-442f23b68b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e659d8d0-8e45-530b-8b4a-442f23b68b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369416} :answer-field/id #uuid "271bb36d-b127-5fcf-a36f-9147b6346c34" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2fa42b4-a390-5778-9455-bd101ae419fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333654" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+{y}^{3}={x}^{3},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+{y}^{3} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={x}^{3}+x{y}^{′}-{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(0)={y}^{′}(0)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(0)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3},\\, & z(0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=1,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3} \\\\  & ={0}^{3}+0\\cdot 1-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd3b7856-4ac5-5473-a585-949dd06100e8" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3953f0a-7cb1-589a-b69d-2f7aa0020c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3953f0a-7cb1-589a-b69d-2f7aa0020c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369419} :answer-field/id #uuid "e49b37fe-7744-5251-95d9-e552403404b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369418} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8421e429-adf2-5700-b463-c829f121ce4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8421e429-adf2-5700-b463-c829f121ce4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369421} :answer-field/id #uuid "da6e53c7-179d-5ef5-af99-2d9d5ae3139d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "273ff591-3e8d-5e11-81b1-07f09d198708" :question/math-academy-id "q-333710" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}-3y{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}-3y{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3}{2}-1=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=z(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd3b7856-4ac5-5473-a585-949dd06100e8" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d4e871f-f21d-518b-8cd6-e88404a8fd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369422} {:answer/id #uuid "767bedcf-f356-558c-8ece-0f05e3433ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "767bedcf-f356-558c-8ece-0f05e3433ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369423} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9572349-9e73-5f53-87a2-625332ffbd62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220542} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "631f173c-3d6d-5773-bd2b-f67bd9ecff22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "631f173c-3d6d-5773-bd2b-f67bd9ecff22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369424} :answer-field/id #uuid "f019e598-fb2a-53d7-b68e-cfae193834c4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5d59cc7-44b7-5133-bc4e-ec03309ee9ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-333724" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+2y{y}^{′}=3x,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+2y{y}^{′} & =3x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =3x-2y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{3x-2y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{3x-2yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{3x-2yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{3x-2yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $5$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=(-1)+5=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈0$\nand ${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd3b7856-4ac5-5473-a585-949dd06100e8" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6feb15e3-072f-5c73-b1ef-d5c785fbe36e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6feb15e3-072f-5c73-b1ef-d5c785fbe36e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369427} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f49825-68be-5e9c-a991-309d843d5da2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369426} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b64a02-ed4e-56ec-a635-13ec15a13ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b64a02-ed4e-56ec-a635-13ec15a13ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369429} :answer-field/id #uuid "275b1906-bc8a-5a66-a2b6-a42339f9fcdf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8f74056-bfdb-5b36-85b6-d5e62833e765" :question/math-academy-id "q-333817" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ y{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}+4x{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4x{y}^{′}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y},\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 0}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈1$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd3b7856-4ac5-5473-a585-949dd06100e8" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7aea145-0879-5d1f-b0fa-806fcc819203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369432} {:answer/id #uuid "39e1229a-6c14-5ad0-b316-af2a299e3cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7aea145-0879-5d1f-b0fa-806fcc819203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369432} :answer-field/id #uuid "af3628eb-8cd7-57b7-b508-e48099a0aec9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d146bd-c7f4-5a8b-b705-fe794b58ff55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56d146bd-c7f4-5a8b-b705-fe794b58ff55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369435} :answer-field/id #uuid "8adc27d1-fb26-5129-b368-6b1d7dd4e01c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "325786e9-8c04-5627-916c-dc2d75122de8" :question/math-academy-id "q-333822" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n3y{y}^{″}-({y}^{′})^{2}=4x,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{y}^{″}-({y}^{′})^{2} & =4x \\\\ 3y{y}^{″} & =4x+({y}^{′})^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4x+({y}^{′})^{2}}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{5}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |  | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈2$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{8}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab3794e-d596-5da5-80df-80c8679b9929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369438} {:answer/id #uuid "3d11c29d-1379-52bc-9d4f-e73c6325ac43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369439} {:answer/id #uuid "571a1f98-46a3-5301-966f-2045608ee7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "571a1f98-46a3-5301-966f-2045608ee7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369440} :answer-field/id #uuid "c412ab25-253c-5917-99c9-394e24f8e5af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369437} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad532b7-7c85-5fdd-a566-de79f4f79244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369442} {:answer/id #uuid "2ccd0532-61cb-5b48-9864-7a44a18c6a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369443} {:answer/id #uuid "f2eb424e-67c6-52ab-8f12-b69d40e271cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2eb424e-67c6-52ab-8f12-b69d40e271cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369444} :answer-field/id #uuid "43cdbae4-f86a-5c61-a08c-9d6e6e93bfde" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7341746b-6ccf-5adc-9603-b713cd37b56b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333875" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-2y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2y-x{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2y-x{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{4})}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{18}=\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{9}$ | $\\frac{5}{18}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |  |  | $\\frac{25}{9}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈\\frac{15}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{25}{9}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4dbe8cd-40fd-5638-8ce6-8f1bd9df15c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369447} {:answer/id #uuid "d71c6cad-e8cb-5b32-8559-33f84792b134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369448} {:answer/id #uuid "8fde0c6c-0b0e-5249-9b2b-0162e59736aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d71c6cad-e8cb-5b32-8559-33f84792b134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369448} :answer-field/id #uuid "1590ed65-c580-53d2-b255-e0a4b498743f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "444fce5f-216c-5d12-9244-5c502f77d272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369451} {:answer/id #uuid "d9772257-02b5-5ddf-a256-d0ed661002b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369452} {:answer/id #uuid "5d839dbe-ffc0-5b5b-b6bf-553d61fa7b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9772257-02b5-5ddf-a256-d0ed661002b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369452} :answer-field/id #uuid "d10d214b-d870-54c8-ba70-cf082d7ba4ef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45d8fb92-b013-52d1-8dce-907150e07f98" :question/math-academy-id "q-333994" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and ${y}^{′}(3).$\n\n$$\ny(3)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(3)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+2y & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =4{y}^{′}-2y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4{y}^{′}-2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{4z-2y}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4z-2y}{x},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4z-2y}{x}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)}{1}=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot 1=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+6=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |  |  | $7$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=7 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4z-2y}{x}=\\frac{4\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 0}{2}=14 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=7\\cdot 1=7 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=14\\cdot 1=14 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+7=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=7+14=21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $7$ | $7$ | $14$ | $14$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $21$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈7$\nand\n${y}^{′}(3)=z(3)≈21.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88a692ee-64a4-5465-885a-bdbb0709e8ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369456} {:answer/id #uuid "738b8226-77d4-5ae5-994e-5e237f42c657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369457} {:answer/id #uuid "11a69772-1bf9-5fc3-a5f2-5e5f3b9b912f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "738b8226-77d4-5ae5-994e-5e237f42c657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369457} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d97f477-b7a4-55dc-a84d-6967f2c7cf1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f390e5f-9c64-5486-afa1-d6e185efd603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369460} {:answer/id #uuid "56271921-1bdd-513e-ab27-4d73fbc4ecf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369461} {:answer/id #uuid "07832d89-df65-51f5-b4ac-980c7d82fe73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{41}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f390e5f-9c64-5486-afa1-d6e185efd603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369460} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5ecab43-8685-5d11-9ea9-2fa992444f6f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcd8e866-1dd2-537e-9fbd-00325b010c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-333997" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n2x{y}^{″}+5({y}^{′})^{2}+x=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and ${y}^{′}(3).$\n\n$$\ny(3)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(3)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{″}+5({y}^{′})^{2}+x & =0 \\\\ 2x{y}^{″} & =-5({y}^{′})^{2}-x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{5({y}^{′})^{2}+x}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x},\\, & z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=-1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}=-\\frac{5\\cdot (-1)^{2}+1}{2\\cdot 1}=-3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-3\\cdot 1=-3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2-1=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1-3=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}=-\\frac{5\\cdot (-4)^{2}+2}{2\\cdot 2}=-\\frac{41}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-4\\cdot 1=-4 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{41}{2}\\cdot 1=-\\frac{41}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1-4=-3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-4-\\frac{41}{2}=-\\frac{49}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $-\\frac{41}{2}$ | $-\\frac{41}{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ |  |  | $-\\frac{49}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-3$\nand\n${y}^{′}(3)=z(3)≈-\\frac{49}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7308a9e-70c1-5a26-a72a-cfddc38b83d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369465} {:answer/id #uuid "941ba567-b5b4-5d36-99ff-2397de8e235f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369466} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f84225-f4e4-5b76-a7f4-8df05cff74fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "941ba567-b5b4-5d36-99ff-2397de8e235f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369466} :answer-field/id #uuid "822056ab-1455-5747-aa00-c3cf79a2db2e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369464} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5c7f74b-2b91-5d86-b060-d2d6cc049350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369469} {:answer/id #uuid "e2826587-8656-5b71-b972-b31093c26771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369470} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0fd00b-4819-5bfa-8216-39690e9ac91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5c7f74b-2b91-5d86-b060-d2d6cc049350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369469} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d84b38e-0b45-5b5d-aa7f-f16fd7de74f6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dccca83f-6ee2-5c3d-8c84-954659378f9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334003" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-x=0,\\,y(-1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=-2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-x & =0 \\\\ y{y}^{″} & =x-3{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{x-3{y}^{′}}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{x-3z}{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(-1)={y}^{′}(-1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(-1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{x-3z}{y},\\, & z(-1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=-1$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=1$ and $z(-1)=-2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x-3z}{y}=\\frac{-1-3(-2)}{1}=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-2\\cdot 1=-2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot 1=5 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1-2=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+5=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x-3z}{y}=\\frac{0-3\\cdot 3}{(-1)}=9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=9\\cdot 1=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=3+9=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $9$ | $9$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈2$\nand\n${y}^{′}(1)=z(1)≈12.$"}]}
